ate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities; concerns: show-off, not open to new ideas. No direct evidence on stress resilience, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, adaptability.\n\nB: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills; concerns: weak memory for numbers, nasty remarks, grumpy, uncooperative. Also we have no direct evidence on adaptability.\n\nWe have new info from Agent 1 about B's decision quality (weather assessment) and attention (conscientious), crew cooperation concerns (grumpy, uncooperative). No adaptability.\n\nNow we need to decide. The panel hasn't discussed C and D yet. We should perhaps still keep A as provisional, but we need to address the new argument from Agent 3. We could say we still favor A because its strengths in anticipation and leadership are more critical, and the concerns about adaptability are less severe than B's crew cooperation issues (nasty remarks). Or we could shift to B. Let's weigh.\n\nA's strengths: anticipates dangerous situations (situational awareness), sees complex connections (cognitive), excellent spatial vision (attention), leadership (crew cooperation). Concerns: show-off (crew trust), not open to new ideas (adaptability). No direct evidence on stress resilience, decision quality, attention to detail (though spatial vision suggests attention). B's strengths: stress resilience, decision quality (weather assessment), conscientiousness (attention), computer skills (technical). Concerns: grumpy, uncooperative, nasty remarks (crew cooperation), weak memory for numbers (attention). Also no adaptability info.\n\nBoth have crew cooperation concerns. B's are more explicit negative (nasty remarks). A's are more about attitude (show-off) and not open to new ideas. Which is more severe? Possibly B's nasty remarks could be more detrimental to crew environment. A's show-off may be less severe. Also A's anticipation of dangerous situations is a unique strength for long-distance flights.\n\nThus I might stay with A, but need to argue why A still preferable. I can say that while B shows strong stress resilience and decision quality, the lack of evidence on adaptability and the serious crew cooperation concerns (nasty remarks) outweigh those strengths, especially given A's leadership and situational awareness. So I keep recommendation for A.\n\nThus my public message: I acknowledge Agent 3's shift, but I remain with A, citing evidence and concerns. Also note we still need to discuss C and D.\n\nNow produce METADATA_JSON with vote \"Candidate A\".\n\nProceed."}
